  <p>The <a href="/resources/specs">Platform specs hub</a> covers other destinations. These requirements apply to Shopify product media; don’t treat them as a specification for every channel where you sell.</p>

  <h2>Why do Shopify’s own image limits differ between pages?</h2>

  <p>The product-media page and the Files table agree on 25 MP. The theme-image page has a 20 MP upload-limit section, while another passage on that page mentions 25 MP. It also says product-image limits differ. That leaves a documented spread of 20 to 25 MP across these contexts, not a reason to replace the product-specific ceiling with the smaller number. The product page says smaller than 20 MB; the Files page says a maximum of 20 MB. Stay below that boundary to satisfy both wordings.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The theme-image page contains both 20 MP and 25 MP guidance. Method: compare its upload-limit section with its compression section and the product-specific table. This is a documentation conflict, not a measured acceptance test.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Uploading images, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/images/theme-images</p>

  <p>Before exporting the catalog, try a file in the intended upload location. Record the upload location, exported dimensions, format, and file weight alongside the result. If a large image is rejected, preserve the error text and make a smaller export for that placement. Keep the original master.</p>

  <p>The accepted-format lists need the same care. Product media types also names TIFF, BMP, and SVG. Uploading images says TIFF and BMP convert on upload; resizing or cropping SVG needs raster output. For a routine photo handoff, use JPEG for a photographic export or PNG when you need transparency, then inspect the uploaded result. This follows the practical format guidance in Shopify’s Uploading images page. Product media types puts PNG first, then JPEG, so don’t read either preference as an exclusive acceptance list.</p>

  <h2>Who decides whether the image is cropped?</h2>

  <p>Your file sets the available picture. The theme sets how that picture fits its display area. Shopify’s media-editor guidance recommends matching aspect ratios for images shown together. Its theme troubleshooting page gives the same advice for collection images that don’t align.</p>

