  <p>Within a product set, a common ratio makes changes of view less distracting. Treat that as a production choice. Shopify doesn’t require every image to be square.</p>

  <p>As a framing example, picture a tall bottle beside a wide serving bowl. A square canvas can hold both, but their empty space will differ. Approve their apparent scale in the collection grid before applying the same crop to the rest of the range. The bowl may need more space around it than you expected.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Matching aspect ratios helps collection images align. Resizing with the proportions unlocked can distort a product; crop to change the shape. Method: read Shopify’s editor instructions and collection troubleshooting.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Using the media editor and Troubleshooting your online store theme, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/shopify-admin/productivity-tools/media-editor https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/themes/theme-support/troubleshooting</p>

  <p>In the theme editor, inspect the affected section’s image settings. Their names and availability depend on the theme and version. Check whether the section can adapt to the image, or whether a supported focal point keeps the important detail in frame.</p>

  <p>Preview the product page and collection separately. The collection card may hide an edge that the product page shows.</p>

  <h2>Should you compress every image to a fixed file weight?</h2>

  <p>No. Shopify’s photography guide suggests aiming at 200 KB or less. Its theme-image guide says manual compression isn’t necessary and recommends uploading the best quality available. The small-file suggestion is advice, not the product upload ceiling.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The photography guide’s 200 KB target is an optimization suggestion. Method: read the recommendation in its photography context, rather than treating it as an upload rule.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Taking product photographs, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/product-media/product-photography The different best-quality upload advice appears in Uploading images, also read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/images/theme-images</p>

  <p>Stay inside the file-weight ceiling, then look closely at the export. If compression makes a knit look waxy or erases an embossed mark, increase quality and compare again. Lettering is worth checking at full size, even when the rest of the picture looks fine in the collection grid.</p>

  <p>Open the larger preview.</p>

  <p>Shopify can deliver a different image format to a browser. That delivery choice doesn’t mean every format it serves belongs on the upload list.</p>

  <h2>What should the filename and alt text say?</h2>

  <p>Use a unique, descriptive filename, for example linen-tote-natural-side.jpg. Shopify warns against starting a filename with a period and against endings reserved for image transformations. Avoid an underscore immediately before dimensions in a filename. These naming rules help avoid unintended filename changes.</p>

  <p>For alt text, describe the visible product and view. In this case, “Natural linen tote shown from the side with its outer pocket” tells the reader something useful. A string of sales adjectives does not. Change the description when the image shows a different detail rather than copying the same text across the gallery.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Use descriptive filenames and alt text that describes what the image shows. Method: read the Files naming guidance and Shopify’s SEO alt-text instructions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Uploading and managing files and Adding keywords for SEO, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/shopify-admin/productivity-tools/file-uploads https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/seo/adding-keywords</p>

  <h2>What changes when you add video or a model?</h2>

  <p>The video limits in the opening table apply to uploaded files. Shopify’s product page gives a 4K maximum of 4096 x 2160 pixels; its Files page says UHD and recommends 3840 x 2160. For a landscape handoff, use the documented UHD recommendation unless your upload test establishes another accepted export. Keep the source clip separately.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The opening table uses the Files page’s uploaded-video limits. The export distinction above compares its UHD recommendation with the product page. Method: read both Help Center pages without merging their resolution wording.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Product media types and Uploading and managing files, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/product-media/product-media-types https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/shopify-admin/productivity-tools/file-uploads</p>

  <p>Confirm that the theme supports the media, and check the store’s remaining capacity: video and model allowances depend on its plan. For a model, inspect its materials and orientation in the storefront after upload.</p>

  <h2>How do you prepare the image in hubStudio?</h2>

  <p>Once the destination and crop are settled, prepare the export.</p>

  <p>Start with a clear photograph of the actual product. Use Edit an image for an instructed change, or Upscale &amp; restore for an older source. Image studio supports renders up to 4K depending on the engine. Review the result against the original, especially seams and fittings that the edit could have changed.</p>

  <p>Open the result in the Image editor. In Crop, choose Square 1:1, position the box, and apply the crop. In Save, choose PNG, JPG, or WEBP and set the size. The 2048 px long-side shortcut appears when the source is larger; the width and height remain proportional. A square crop gives a square export. The editor works at a maximum long side of 4096 pixels, so keep larger originals separately.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Image studio offers up to 4K on supported engines. The Image editor has Square 1:1 cropping, a conditional 2048 px shortcut, and a 4096 px working limit. Method: checked against the documented controls, not an engine test.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio Help, Create an image and Assets Library and the tools, checked October 20, 2026. https://www.hubstudio.ai/help/create-an-image https://www.hubstudio.ai/help/assets-library</p>

  <p>Save a copy in the Assets Library if you want the edited file kept there, or use Download for a local export. Upload the result through the product’s Media section in Shopify. hubStudio doesn’t connect or sync to Shopify.</p>

  <p>If the upload fails, check the real file format as well as its extension. Shopify’s Adding product media guide describes failures caused by a mismatch. Renaming the extension isn’t a conversion.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Upload product media from the product’s Media section; check format mismatches when a video lacks a working preview. Method: read the upload steps and troubleshooting entries.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shopify Help Center, Adding product media, undated, read October 20, 2026. https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/product-media/add-media</p>

